E. Business Question 3
Which content categories perform best?

Because the CSV contains category_id rather than a readable category name, the SQL analysis should map the IDs to category names.

A recruiter-friendly approach is a CTE mapping:

WITH category_map AS (
    SELECT *
    FROM (
        VALUES
            (1, 'Film & Animation'),
            (2, 'Autos & Vehicles'),
            (10, 'Music'),
            (15, 'Pets & Animals'),
            (17, 'Sports'),
            (19, 'Travel & Events'),
            (20, 'Gaming'),
            (22, 'People & Blogs'),
            (23, 'Comedy'),
            (24, 'Entertainment'),
            (25, 'News & Politics'),
            (26, 'Howto & Style'),
            (27, 'Education'),
            (28, 'Science & Technology'),
            (29, 'Nonprofits & Activism'),
            (43, 'Shows')
    ) AS x(category_id, category_name)
),

latest_video AS (
    SELECT *,
           ROW_NUMBER() OVER (
               PARTITION BY video_id
               ORDER BY trending_date DESC
           ) AS rn
    FROM usvideos_clean
)

SELECT
    c.category_name,
    COUNT(DISTINCT v.video_id) AS videos,
    SUM(v.views) AS total_views,
    SUM(v.likes) AS total_likes,
    SUM(v.comment_count) AS total_comments,
    ROUND(
        100.0 * SUM(v.likes + v.comment_count)
        / NULLIF(SUM(v.views), 0),
        2
    ) AS engagement_rate_pct
FROM latest_video v
JOIN category_map c
    ON v.category_id = c.category_id
WHERE v.rn = 1
GROUP BY c.category_name
ORDER BY total_views DESC;
Why this is used

This demonstrates:

