F. Business Question 4
What patterns can we see in trending/discovery activity over time?
SELECT
    trending_date,
    COUNT(DISTINCT video_id) AS unique_trending_videos,
    COUNT(DISTINCT channel_title) AS active_creators,
    COUNT(*) AS trending_observations
FROM usvideos_clean
GROUP BY trending_date
ORDER BY trending_date;
Why this is used

This measures the discovery layer of the platform.

Instead of asking only:

"What videos have the most views?"

we ask:

"What does the trending ecosystem look like over time?"

This is much closer to an AICines creator/content-platform problem.

Actual finding

The dataset covers 205 observed trending dates from November 2017 through June 2018.

Most days contain approximately 200 trending observations, with the observed daily range being 196–200.

That means raw daily volume is structurally constrained and should not be interpreted as organic audience demand by itself.

For Power BI, the useful visualization is therefore a time-series showing:

unique trending videos
active creators
trending observations

rather than presenting raw row count as a popularity KPI.

G. Business Question 5
Which creators consistently produce content that trends?

First determine how long each video remained in the trending dataset:

WITH video_trending AS (
    SELECT
        video_id,
        channel_title,
        title,
        COUNT(DISTINCT trending_date) AS trending_days
    FROM usvideos_clean
    GROUP BY
        video_id,
        channel_title,
        title
)

SELECT
    video_id,
    title,
    channel_title,
    trending_days
FROM video_trending
ORDER BY trending_days DESC
LIMIT 20;
Why this is used

This separates one-time viral success from sustained discovery.

Actual finding

The longest observed trending durations include:

Sam Smith — 29 days
Lucas and Marcus — 29 days
grav3yardgirl — 28 days
20th Century Fox — 28 days
Complex — 28 days
Creator-level consistency
WITH video_trending AS (
    SELECT
        video_id,
        channel_title,
        COUNT(DISTINCT trending_date) AS trending_days
    FROM usvideos_clean
    GROUP BY
        video_id,
        channel_title
),

creator_consistency AS (
    SELECT
        channel_title,
        COUNT(DISTINCT video_id) AS videos,
        SUM(trending_days) AS total_trending_video_days,
        ROUND(AVG(trending_days), 2) AS avg_trending_days_per_video,
        MAX(trending_days) AS longest_trending_duration
    FROM video_trending
    GROUP BY channel_title
)

SELECT
    channel_title,
    videos,
    total_trending_video_days,
    avg_trending_days_per_video,
    longest_trending_duration
FROM creator_consistency
WHERE videos >= 3
ORDER BY
    avg_trending_days_per_video DESC,
    longest_trending_duration DESC;
Why the videos >= 3 condition matters

Without a minimum volume requirement, a creator with one unusually successful video could dominate a consistency analysis.

This is a good example of analytical judgment, not just SQL syntax.

The dataset shows creators such as zefrank1, Gibi ASMR, Bleacher Report, and VH1 with high average trending duration among creators with at least three observed videos.

H. Business Question 6
How strong is audience engagement relative to content reach?
WITH latest_video AS (
    SELECT
        *,
        ROW_NUMBER() OVER (
            PARTITION BY video_id
            ORDER BY trending_date DESC
        ) AS rn
    FROM usvideos_clean
)

SELECT
    video_id,
    title,
    channel_title,
    views,
    likes,
    comment_count,

    ROUND(
        100.0 * likes / NULLIF(views, 0),
        2
    ) AS like_rate_pct,

    ROUND(
        100.0 * comment_count / NULLIF(views, 0),
        2
    ) AS comment_rate_pct,

    ROUND(
        100.0 * (likes + comment_count)
        / NULLIF(views, 0),
        2
    ) AS engagement_rate_pct

FROM latest_video
WHERE rn = 1
ORDER BY engagement_rate_pct DESC;
Why this is used

This creates three useful product/content KPIs:

Like Rate

likes ÷ views × 100

Comment Rate

comments ÷ views × 100

Overall Engagement Rate

(likes + comments) ÷ views × 100

This is more informative than views alone.

The overall latest-snapshot dataset has approximately:

12.46B views
352.96M likes
40.97M comments
3.16% overall engagement rate



